+# DevInsight AI — request-flow governance final
+
+30 October 2026, Asia/Karachi. Source/current branch: `task/profile`; integration/PR target: not applicable. Baseline: `c612ae80c466f47ab7def651da6537d8cf330b0a`; initial index and checkout were clean. No commit, deployment, infrastructure/configuration change, secret exposure/change, destructive migration or historical-record change was performed.
+
+**Verdict: local implementation and focused verification PASS — 117 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped; production activation remains gated.** This change removes Redis as an admission dependency for Profile/Portfolio. It does not establish fleet-wide security for unrelated modules, activate B2 or freeze Profile. The stable Profile/B2 contracts and historical acceptance reports remain intact.
+
+## Decision and evidence boundaries
+
+Read the root `AGENTS.md`, architecture charter (especially ADR-14–16), Integrations Mongo governance implementation and acceptance, Profile implementation/B2/security acceptance, Profile development handoff, storage operations and affected controllers/middleware. The plan was: establish the clean branch baseline; inventory mounted request paths and service authorities; reuse the tested Mongo governance machinery; exercise failures/concurrency/authenticated storage with disposable fixtures; format explicit files, retain evidence, inspect exact diff/cleanup and record release gates.
+
+The initial outside-workspace approval review failed because its review model was at capacity. The original checkout was untouched while a candidate was prepared in the chat workspace. After the user requested retry, approval succeeded and focused changes were transferred to the original checkout after a fresh branch/HEAD/clean-status check. Subsequent implementation and final verification use the original checkout. No approval or security check was bypassed.
+
+The companion `REQUEST-ENDPOINT-INVENTORY.md` and `request-endpoint-inventory.json` record 247 mounted route declarations across 36 router mounts, including six nested Recruiter Hub mounts, source line numbers, router-wide guards and ordered handler expressions. Two Jobs templates each expand across save/apply/hide, yielding 251 mounted API method/path combinations. Root `/`, `/metrics` and `/uploads` are described separately in the global flow. These counts distinguish source tracing from runtime coverage. The matrix below supplies service-layer branches; the focused fixture tests exercise the changed paths. Unrelated endpoints were source-audited, not all exercised through live services.
+
+## Global execution order
+
+`backend/index.js`: skill-gap timing marker → Helmet → CORS → global API limiter → request context → bounded JSON/webhook-byte capture (100 kb) → bounded URL-encoded parser (100 kb) → maintenance guard → `/uploads` access/storage/static chain → HTTP metrics → optional request logging → audit middleware → mounted route/router guards → controller/service → error middleware. Multipart bodies are not parsed by the global JSON middleware. CORS preflight may terminate before later middleware. Maintenance exemptions remain unchanged. Redis startup is optional and bounded to four seconds; no new startup dependency was added.
+
+The global limiter precedes persisted authentication. Its verified JWT identity is only an ingress quota key; it does not authorize anything. Route `protect` independently verifies issuer/audience/session revision, loads the Mongo account and checks active status. Owner predicates and storage ledger checks remain authoritative. Existing authentication catches a failed account lookup as authentication failure; a complete database outage is therefore not guaranteed to reach admission or produce its 503. A failure specifically in Mongo admission produces 503 before protected work, as tested.
+
+## Before/after flows by affected endpoint
+
+All rows inherit the global prefix above. `A` = persisted authentication; `M` = media-session cookie middleware; `Q` = fixed shared admission; `O` = current Mongo ownership/publication checks. No file/provider work is inside an admission transaction.
+
